\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {A learned lifting policy cuts lift time from 1.00\,s to 0.37\,s, and a physics shield is what keeps it safe.} (a) The same object lifted by the unshielded residual policy (B2), which slips 21\,mm in the fingers (the rod falls below the red mark on the palm), and by the shield-trained policy (B5), which does not. (b) Unshielded, the policy fails 12--16\% of lifts once kinetic friction is lower than static ($r>1$); shielded, it never fails, even beyond the shield's assumed $r_{\max }=1.2$ (40 test objects, 5 seeds, Wilson 95\% intervals). (c) Every learned variant is 2.7$\times $ faster than the friction-model controller but grips harder. (d) Letting the shield allow some slip saves grip only if friction is assumed not to drop.}}{1}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:teaser}{{1}{1}{\textbf {A learned lifting policy cuts lift time from 1.00\,s to 0.37\,s, and a physics shield is what keeps it safe.} (a) The same object lifted by the unshielded residual policy (B2), which slips 21\,mm in the fingers (the rod falls below the red mark on the palm), and by the shield-trained policy (B5), which does not. (b) Unshielded, the policy fails 12--16\% of lifts once kinetic friction is lower than static ($r>1$); shielded, it never fails, even beyond the shield's assumed $r_{\max }=1.2$ (40 test objects, 5 seeds, Wilson 95\% intervals). (c) Every learned variant is 2.7$\times $ faster than the friction-model controller but grips harder. (d) Letting the shield allow some slip saves grip only if friction is assumed not to drop}{figure.1}{}}
